4. Runbooks: Troubleshooting & Disaster Scenarios
Scenario 1: SSLV3_ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE / Connection Dropped Instantly
Symptom: Upstream clients fail to establish connection boundaries. API returns zero responses.
Root Cause: The calling framework didn't supply an mTLS client certificate signed by the designated ca.crt pool configuration mounted inside the uvicorn gateway container.
Resolution Steps:
Inspect client-side connection arguments. Confirm client.crt and client.key are explicitly populated within the outgoing execution session.
Inspect certificate validity constraints: openssl x509 -in certs/client.crt -text -noout to make sure it is not expired.
Verify the server configuration inside docker-compose.yml. Ensure the path /app/certs/ca.crt correctly points to the root CA file matching the client certificate.
Scenario 2: Celery Sandbox Pipeline Stays PENDING / Timeout Failures
Symptom: Gateway accepts calls, provides a valid task_id, but queries to /api/v1/tasks/{task_id} run indefinitely or timeout.
Root Cause: The celery-worker container lost communication permissions with the internal Docker daemon socket (/var/run/docker.sock), preventing it from spinning up ephemeral containers.
Resolution Steps:
Check worker logs: docker-compose logs celery-worker. Look for permissions errors or an unreachable host error.
Verify your filesystem permissions: ls -l /var/run/docker.sock. If the socket permissions are restricted on your host machine, grant the container permission to use it:
sudo chmod 666 /var/run/docker.sock


Restart the background tasks engine to re-establish connection pipes:
docker-compose restart celery-worker


Scenario 3: Llama Guard Node Crash / 502 Safety Infrastructure Offline
Symptom: Valid processing transactions generate immediate 502 application errors.
Root Cause: Your classification server endpoint defined under LLAMA_GUARD_ENDPOINT crashed or ran out of VRAM, causing your firewall's fail-closed protection to block all incoming payloads.
Resolution Steps:
If you are using a mock endpoint, check that it's online. If you are using an internal vLLM cluster, run a manual health check:
curl -I http://internal.local


If the node is down due to a VRAM Out-of-Memory (OOM) error, restart the inference runner using context length allocation flags to free up memory:
python -m vllm.entrypoints.openai.api_server --model meta-llama/Llama-Guard-3-8B --max-model-len 4096


If you need to temporarily bypass this layer during an outage, clear the LLAMA_GUARD_ENDPOINT environment variable in your docker-compose.yml configuration and restart the container stack.
Scenario 4: Storage Exhaustion Failure / Worker Container Out of Disk Space
Symptom: Background task workers throw container instantiation failures (no space left on device).
Root Cause: The worker pulled and tore down sandbox images repeatedly, but the host engine left dangling filesystem metadata layers behind, filling up your storage.
Resolution Steps:
Log into the node host cluster engine and clean out unused resources:
docker system prune -af --volumes


Add a cron job to your base operating system script to run an automated cleanup at midnight every night:
echo "0 0 * * * root docker system prune -af --volumes" | sudo tee -a /etc/crontab


Scenario 5: AWS WAF False Positive Event / System Admin Locked Out
Symptom: Trusted enterprise nodes receive 403 Forbidden messages directly from the AWS ALB layer before matching application constraints.
Root Cause: An internal engineer ran an integration test script that triggered the CloudWatch Metric Filter alarm, causing the automation Lambda to block the office IP address.
Resolution Steps:
Open your terminal with active AWS administrator access tokens.
Fetch the target IP blocklist definition metadata context:
aws wafv2 get-ip-set --name ai-gateway-attacker-blocklist --scope REGIONAL --id <YOUR-IP-SET-ID>


Update the AWS WAF IP set to remove your office IP address using the console or the AWS CLI, clearing the lock out instantly.
